{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/686899-changeling-s-theme-eludes-me","id":686899,"title":"Changeling's theme eludes me","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-10-03T06:17:50-07:00","id":686899,"title":"Changeling's theme eludes me","content":"Just so we're clear, I'm not Trolling...I really don't get it, but I want to. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> From what I've read in the core book, Changeling has a pretty strong abuse/rape metiphor (or not so metaphor) as its central theme. A community of being who are united against their abusers (the true fey) slowly going mad.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I know this is WoD, so &quot;levity&quot; is in short supply, but I don't know how to make a community that's basically a giant rape-crisis centre fun. It's just so blunt and on-the-nose its damn uncomfortable. How do you make that engaging? <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> My players are jazzed to play it, so they clearly get something I don't. I asked them where they see their enjoyment coming from, but I can't get a solid answer (I think they're just looking forward to using contracts). <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Reaching out to my fellow storytellers and players...how do I take such a harsh theme and build a chronicle that won't be miserable?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4656-megaarcon","name":"MegaArcon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4656_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-03T06:36:12-07:00","id":686937,"title":"","content":"The qualifier you might need is that it's a story about abuse <i>survivors</i>. The abuse itself is the durance, the backstory. The game is about what you do now, having escaped that abuse, about building a new life for yourself, about dealing with how you've changed. You have a new beginning - what are you going to do with it?<br /> <br /> It's also about learning about the new world you've found yourself in, the world of Faerie and its magic. Making best use of how you've changed, how you now engage with the world.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-03T07:10:35-07:00","id":686951,"title":"","content":"I\u2019ve seen Changeling described as a game about finding home and I think that really is the core of it. I won\u2019t deny that the abuse survivor angle is a core part of the game, because the game fundamentally has an element of \u2018balance\u2019 between your human and fae sides. It\u2019s about coming to terms with what you\u2019ve been through and how it changed you and finding out who you are now. Do you go back to your family, killing your fetch, and try to return to a world that went on without you? Do you try to connect with your fetch (if your ST likes the idea)? Do you abandon your old life and start a new one? Do you abandon your human identity entirely and become the crazy old witch in the hedge-hollow? I think the game as written makes it sound like there are wrong choices, and it emphasises a healthy balance, but I personally see total rejection of the fae world or human world as valid\u2026 though naturally both should come with some consequences.<br /> <br /> Making a chronicle that isn\u2019t miserable takes a bit of forethought on the part of the ST \u2013 Promethean is an incredibly bleak game about hope. Changeling can be the same. Ensure that hope is a part of every game session. Maybe the changeling killer with shredded Clarity the PCs hunt down can be spared and helped. The frenzied hobgoblin can be tamed or negotiated with. The Gentry can be outsmarted, especially by playing to their weaknesses (especially their lack of empathy \u2013 True Fae will never, ever understand altruistic behaviour, and this can be fun to play with).<br /> <br /> This even applies to the magic side. Fae magic is quite nasty, based around costs, but if you\u2019re prepared to meet the price? Anything is possible. The Hedge-plant with its writhing thorns and butcher-bird servitors hides a Goblin fruit that can heal a terminal cancer. Goblin Markets sell anything you can possibly imagine. A True Fae can offer you almost anything if you defeat it and it begs for mercy, and it will <i>never</i> break its word. You can make it swear to leave you alone. Leave earth alone for a century. Protect you from other True Fae.<br /> <br /> Becoming a changeling is horrible. Being a changeling is difficult. There\u2019s a lot of nastiness in your past threatening to catch up with you. There are difficulties in your present, challenges unique to the Lost\u2026 But your future is yours. Make your own happy ending.<br /> <br /> Also feel free to downplay the madness elements if you find them troublesome or difficult. It\u2019s your game and you clearly have some issues. If you can\u2019t resolve them, downplay the elements that make you uncomfortable, and if you still can\u2019t muster enjoyment for the game\u2026 Play something else. If a game makes any player uncomfortable, find another. The same should be true of the ST.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Onyx Path Freelancer: Demon Storyteller's Guide, Dark Eras, Night Horrors: Enemy Action</i><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/825063-storyteller-s-guide-extra-material\" target=\"_blank\">Storyteller's Guide Extra Material</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/899423-after-the-fall-bonus-material\" target=\"_blank\">After the Fall: Bonus Material</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/452-eremite","name":"Eremite","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar452_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"355"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-03T07:39:59-07:00","id":686978,"title":"","content":"I would put forth that Changeling's metaphor is not necessarily about survivors of abuse or rape specifically. It's about survivors of trauma, and abuse is one form that trauma could take. I also think that particular aspect of the game is a bit less on the nose than you might be thinking of it as.<br /> <br /> At its heart, Changeling is about playing Alice after she gets back from Wonderland, or Dorothy when she gets back from Oz, or Sarah when she gets back from the Labyrinth. Their experiences in fairy tale worlds were traumatic as hell when you think about it. Alice almost drowned, was put on trial by a murderous queen, threatened with decapitation, not to mention changing size so many times it caused her to have an actual identity crisis (literally, she asks herself if she woke up as someone else that morning, and if she did, then who was she).<br /> <br /> But as traumatic as these experiences were, they were also fantastical. Throughout all of her experiences in Oz, many of them quite wonderful, all Dorothy wanted the whole time was to go back home. To Kansas of all places. She was willing to give up benevolent fairy-godmother-esque ladies in magic bubbles, and horses of different colors, and cities of emerald, and national recognition as a hero for killing two evil witches, and all of her whimsical new friends for miles of empty farm land and probably more tornados. As much as changeling is about people who survived abuse, it's also about people who couldn't quite let go of the mundane. It's about people who, when given the option to spend eternity in a literal fairy story, decided they'd rather go back to this boring shithole and live out the rest of their lives and then die.<br /> <br /> This conflict between the durance as a magical adventure and the durance as traumatizing abuse is where the struggle to maintain Clarity comes from. They're not so much &quot;going mad&quot; as trying to readjust to normal life again, when you've had these experiences. How could Sarah ever completely trust her senses again after the events of a film with the tag line &quot;a place where everything seems wonderful and nothing is as it seems&quot;? I mean, at one point the Labyrinth actually made her think she was back in her bedroom so she would stop trying to reach the center. How can she know for sure that's not still what's happening? Maybe she never really made it back after all. That's the &quot;madness&quot; of changeling is losing your ability to distinguish between fantasy and reality.<br /> <br /> So that's Changeling in a nutshell. You've been through some wonderful and horrible experiences, and then you came back to the mundane world. Now what are you going to do? How do you go back to a normal life after everything that happened, the good and the bad? Well, for one thing, you surround yourself with people who understand (and, for that matter, believe) what you've been through. Maybe together you can hold onto some of the good parts, and help each other move past the bad parts.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 10-03-2015, 10:42 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":11}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-03T09:06:37-07:00","id":687130,"title":"","content":"I think it is hard because it has so many themes.<br /> It is game about abuse.<br /> But also survival.<br /> Trauma.<br /> Madness.<br /> Dreams.<br /> Change.<br /> Growth.<br /> Choice.<br /> Accepting different parts of yourself.<br /> Words.<br /> Prices.<br /> Bargains.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6005-coolcat12345","name":"coolcat12345","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"31"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-03T09:59:11-07:00","id":687208,"title":"","content":"Thanks everyone. Your responses have been incredibility helpful. I'll re-read the material with those new perspectives. The &quot;fluff&quot; in the book double-backs on itself a lot...They went though something terrible, but its beautiful, except when its not. Confused the hell out of me and left me with the sense that I should always keep the terrible past and madness at the forefront. I'm OK with running something dark, but again, that just didn't jive with me. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Hope and a new life are themes I can work with, even if those new lives take a dark turn.<br /> <br /> Thanks again. ^_^","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4656-megaarcon","name":"MegaArcon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4656_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-03T11:27:51-07:00","id":687320,"title":"","content":"Another thing to keep in mind is that the abuse isn't necessarily what you think of in the real world. Going back to the source - fairy tales, fae beings, and faerie madness - helps.<br /> <br /> This wasn't someone being beaten by their significant other, trapped at home, and raped for years necessarily. This was a magical boy you were enthralled with and trusted. He took you and your siblings off for an adventure in Neverland - but being hunted by the Chief wasn't just pretend after all, the pirates could live out their roles with abandon, and then Peter refused to let you go home. Tinkerbelle was no help; she hated you anyway, irrationally, as only a fae can. It was thrilling at times, wonderous at others, and Neverland could be such a heartbreakingly beautiful place. But as time passed and you didn't age, you knew deep in your bones that you didn't belong there. And as Peter put you at ever greater risk just to play his games with the Chief and Hook, you realized that he did not care about you. He couldn't. <br /> <br /> Imagine how an ordinary person might react to becoming Alice, lost in a Wonderland where everything made no sense and so much was threatening at the drop of a dime, apropos of nothing. Even after escape, that person would be changed in many ways and far less trusting, for certain. They would have trouble expecting sanity from anyone, including themself. Worse yet, if agents of Wonderland ventured into the real world after the escapee, that person would be terrified of the madness and chaos they would inevitably bring. And yet Wonderland's games had their fun turns, and it wouldn't be so bad to see the Cheshire Cat again for some of his odd advice...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kismet's <a href=\"http://www.wod.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">WOD</a> - <a href=\"http://www.dnd.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">D&amp;D</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BoundinDreams\" target=\"_blank\">C:tL</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TheNamelessEmpire\" target=\"_blank\">M:tC</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","name":"KismetRose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-06T01:43:16-07:00","id":690968,"title":"","content":"Besides, how many players truly focus on the deeper layers of the theme of the game? I know half of the players in my group love the campaign because they like the mix of fairy tales with a grim modern setting. They love the fact that one day they can visit the fantastic of a goblin market or hunt an actual dragon in the Hedge and the next day they can try and solve who is spreading a dangerous and new designer drug. Durance and fitting back in is just a minor note in the one paragraph background they designed.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4560-madfox11","name":"Madfox11","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"172"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-06T10:18:33-07:00","id":691389,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Madfox11</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/686899-changeling-s-theme-eludes-me?p=690968#post690968\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Besides, how many players truly focus on the deeper layers of the theme of the game? I know half of the players in my group love the campaign because they like the mix of fairy tales with a grim modern setting. They love the fact that one day they can visit the fantastic of a goblin market or hunt an actual dragon in the Hedge and the next day they can try and solve who is spreading a dangerous and new designer drug. Durance and fitting back in is just a minor note in the one paragraph background they designed.</div> </div> </div> </div>I've known players for whom that was the part of Changeling that appealed most to them. I've known players who hated that aspect and saw it as ruining the fun fairytale themes. I've known players who didn't even realize those undertones existed. It varies quite a lot.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}